1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,129
Lesson 1.4.

2
00:00:02,509 --> 00:00:05,110
Maxwell's demon and the Szilard engine.

3
00:00:06,217 --> 00:00:10,313
In 1867 James Clerk Maxwell
imagined a tiny being

4
00:00:10,313 --> 00:00:13,991
guarding a door between
two chambers of gas.

5
00:00:14,371 --> 00:00:17,830
Without doing work, just watching
and opening the door at the

6
00:00:17,830 --> 00:00:21,460
right moment, it seems able to
separate fast molecules from slow

7
00:00:21,460 --> 00:00:24,637
ones and create a temperature
difference out of nothing.

8
00:00:25,017 --> 00:00:28,342
That paradox took more than
a century to resolve, and its

9
00:00:28,342 --> 00:00:31,784
solution is the heart of the
thermodynamics of information.

10
00:00:32,917 --> 00:00:34,577
Watch the demon in action.

11
00:00:34,957 --> 00:00:37,762
When a fast molecule
approaches from the left, it

12
00:00:37,762 --> 00:00:40,510
opens the door and lets
it through to the right.

13
00:00:40,890 --> 00:00:44,893
When a slow one approaches from the
right, it lets it through to the left.

14
00:00:45,273 --> 00:00:49,037
Over time, the right chamber heats
up and the left one cools down.

15
00:00:49,417 --> 00:00:52,611
With that temperature difference
we could drive an engine.

16
00:00:52,991 --> 00:00:55,967
If the demon spends no
energy, we have built a

17
00:00:55,967 --> 00:00:58,814
perpetual motion machine
of the second kind.

18
00:00:59,194 --> 00:01:00,348
Something has to give.

19
00:01:01,450 --> 00:01:05,247
Leó Szilard simplified the
problem to the extreme in

20
00:01:05,247 --> 00:01:08,898
1929: a single molecule
in a box at temperature T.

21
00:01:09,278 --> 00:01:11,467
First a wall is inserted in the middle.

22
00:01:11,847 --> 00:01:14,442
Then the demon looks at
which side the molecule

23
00:01:14,442 --> 00:01:16,761
is on: it acquires one bit of information.

24
00:01:17,141 --> 00:01:21,071
With that bit, it places a piston
on the correct side and lets the

25
00:01:21,071 --> 00:01:25,121
molecule push it while it expands
slowly, taking heat from the bath.

26
00:01:25,501 --> 00:01:28,375
At the end the molecule
occupies the whole box again.

27
00:01:28,755 --> 00:01:31,764
Work has been extracted,
and the cycle can repeat.

28
00:01:32,883 --> 00:01:34,191
Let's compute the work.

29
00:01:34,571 --> 00:01:39,857
For a one-molecule gas, the
equation of state is p V = k_B T.

30
00:01:40,237 --> 00:01:45,715
In the isothermal expansion from half
the volume to the full volume, the

31
00:01:45,715 --> 00:01:50,584
extracted work is the integral
of p dV, which equals k_B T ln 2.

32
00:01:50,964 --> 00:01:54,932
That work comes from the bath's heat:
the internal energy does not change.

33
00:01:55,312 --> 00:02:00,146
One bit of information
became k_B T ln 2 of work.

34
00:02:00,526 --> 00:02:04,162
At one cycle per second, this
seems to violate the second law.

35
00:02:05,283 --> 00:02:09,422
For decades it was thought the
cost lay in measuring: that

36
00:02:09,422 --> 00:02:13,275
observing the molecule
dissipated at least k_B T ln 2.

37
00:02:13,655 --> 00:02:19,623
In 1982 Charles Bennett showed that
measurement can be done reversibly.

38
00:02:20,003 --> 00:02:23,663
The unavoidable cost is
elsewhere: the demon's memory.

39
00:02:24,043 --> 00:02:28,977
To repeat the cycle, the demon must
erase the bit it stored, and by

40
00:02:28,977 --> 00:02:33,321
Landauer's principle, erasing
it costs at least k_B T ln 2.

41
00:02:33,701 --> 00:02:37,184
The balance over the full
cycle is zero at best.

42
00:02:37,564 --> 00:02:40,034
The second law survives, but only if we

43
00:02:40,034 --> 00:02:42,630
count information as a physical quantity.

44
00:02:43,750 --> 00:02:46,073
Let's do the entropy balance of one cycle.

45
00:02:46,453 --> 00:02:50,986
On measuring, the demon's memory
goes from a known state to a bit

46
00:02:50,986 --> 00:02:55,588
correlated with the molecule: the
memory's entropy rises by k_B ln

47
00:02:55,588 --> 00:02:59,493
2, while the gas's uncertainty
drops by the same amount.

48
00:02:59,873 --> 00:03:05,712
During the expansion, the bath delivers
heat and loses k_B ln 2 of entropy.

49
00:03:06,092 --> 00:03:12,306
On erasing, the memory returns to zero
and the bath regains at least k_B ln 2.

50
00:03:12,686 --> 00:03:15,538
Adding everything up, the total
entropy does not decrease.

51
00:03:16,650 --> 00:03:23,227
At three hundred kelvin, each cycle
extracts at most 2.87 × 10⁻²¹ J.

52
00:03:23,607 --> 00:03:26,173
If the measurement has
errors and the demon gets

53
00:03:26,173 --> 00:03:28,685
the side right with ninety
percent probability,

54
00:03:29,065 --> 00:03:34,494
the useful information is only the
mutual information between measurement

55
00:03:34,494 --> 00:03:39,476
and position: one minus the binary
entropy of 0.1, about 0.53 bits.

56
00:03:39,856 --> 00:03:45,060
The maximum extractable work drops to 0.53

57
00:03:45,060 --> 00:03:49,768
times k_B T ln 2, about 1.5 × 10⁻²¹ J.

58
00:03:50,883 --> 00:03:54,976
Real demons exist today:
information engines that use

59
00:03:54,976 --> 00:03:59,222
feedback on colloidal particles
or on single electrons.

60
00:03:59,602 --> 00:04:02,210
With imperfect measurements,
the maximum work

61
00:04:02,210 --> 00:04:04,411
is k_B T times the mutual information.

62
00:04:04,791 --> 00:04:07,758
For computing, the lesson
is clear: measuring and

63
00:04:07,758 --> 00:04:11,511
controlling can be almost free,
but resetting memories is not.

64
00:04:11,891 --> 00:04:16,914
In the lab two challenge you will simulate
a Szilard engine and close the cycle.

65
00:04:18,017 --> 00:04:21,148
The demon connects what we
saw about Shannon entropy

66
00:04:21,148 --> 00:04:23,859
with Landauer's principle
in the next lesson.

67
00:04:24,239 --> 00:04:28,146
In unit three we will see the
generalization of the Jarzynski

68
00:04:28,146 --> 00:04:31,669
equality with feedback and
the thermodynamics of reset.

69
00:04:32,783 --> 00:04:37,148
Frequent mistakes: believing the cost
is in measuring, when it is in erasing.

70
00:04:37,528 --> 00:04:40,221
Thinking the demon violates
the second law, when

71
00:04:40,221 --> 00:04:42,577
what happens is that we forgot its memory.

72
00:04:42,957 --> 00:04:45,265
Forgetting that the
expansion must be slow.

73
00:04:45,645 --> 00:04:48,920
And assuming the full work
when the measurement is noisy.

74
00:04:50,050 --> 00:04:54,322
In summary: Maxwell's demon uses
information to extract work.

75
00:04:54,702 --> 00:04:59,969
The Szilard engine turns one
bit into k_B T ln 2 of work.

76
00:05:00,349 --> 00:05:03,780
The second law survives because
the demon's memory must be

77
00:05:03,780 --> 00:05:06,679
erased, and erasing a bit
costs at least as much.

78
00:05:07,059 --> 00:05:09,482
Information is a thermodynamic resource.

79
00:05:10,583 --> 00:05:13,595
To practice: derive the
work of the expansion.

80
00:05:13,975 --> 00:05:18,077
Compute the average work if the wall
is placed at one third of the volume.

81
00:05:18,457 --> 00:05:20,502
Find the maximum work with a measurement

82
00:05:20,502 --> 00:05:22,597
that is right eighty percent of the time.

83
00:05:22,977 --> 00:05:25,206
And identify the step
where the cost is paid.
